[size=5][b]Tier 7 Expansion[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]

Once a crafting skill is capped, the game stops giving you anything: quality 6 is the ceiling, and every drop from then on is a repeat.

Tier 7 Expansion adds one more step. Everything in the game that has a quality gains a seventh one - a late-game goal to work towards, not a new power level.

[size=4][b]What it covers[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Weapons[/b] - all 43, melee and ranged, junk sledge, turret and drone included
[*][b]Tools[/b] - all 16: axes, picks, shovels, wrenches, claw hammer, nailgun, chainsaw, auger
[*][b]Armour[/b] - all 66 pieces that have a quality
[*][b]Clothing[/b] - all 12, the layer under the armour
[*][b]Item mods[/b] - all 35 that have a quality at all
[/list]

[size=4][b]What tier 7 gives[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Every stat that grows with quality keeps growing[/b] - damage, block damage, durability, damage falloff, physical and elemental resistance, stamina, harvest counts.
[*][b]One more mod slot[/b] than tier 6.
[*][b]Armour set bonuses continue.[/b] A full tier 7 set gets the next step of its set bonus.
[*][b]Item mod effects continue[/b] - bleed and stun chances, magazine size, fuel capacity, the lot.
[*][b]A gold quality band[/b], one step past legendary purple.
[*][b]Crafting skills go to 60, 90 and 120[/b] instead of 50, 75 and 100, so the magazines you find after capping out have somewhere to go.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Balance[/b][/size]

No [i]Ancient Warrior Sword[/i] with 1000 damage here. Tier 7 is the natural continuation of tier 6 - the same step, only with higher requirements - and it is there to stretch the progression once every tier 6 you wanted is already yours.

An AK47 gains +40 durability at tier 7 - the same +40 it gained for every tier before, from 200 at tier 1 to 400 at tier 6. A Raider outfit gains +0.6 damage resistance, again the same as every tier before it. So going from 6 to 7 feels like going from 5 to 6, and nothing below tier 7 changes at all.

What makes it endgame is the cost, not the numbers:

[list]
[*]The crafting skill has to sit at its new cap - a fifth more magazines than tier 6 needed.
[*]A craft takes [b]six Legendary Parts[/b] where tier 6 takes one.
[*]A drop is [b]five times rarer[/b] than tier 6, and no trader ever stocks it.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Where it comes from[/b][/size]

Craft it at the new cap, or find it in loot. A looted one rolls its stats like any looted item, starred band included; a crafted one is plain, exactly like every other crafted quality.

Item mods are loot only - vanilla gives mod recipes no crafting tier at all, so a crafted mod is quality 1 whatever your skill.

Twenty items are treated as junk: pipe guns, primitive weapons, stone tools, primitive armour and the few things with no recipe at all. They get tier 7 free and gain no extra mod slot, because vanilla does not charge a Legendary Part for them at tier 6 either.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]7 Days to Die [b]V 3.2.0[/b]. Built and tested against b9.
[*]The game must run [b]without EasyAntiCheat[/b] - the mod ships a DLL and EAC blocks those.
[*]No other mods needed.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive.
[*]Extract it into your Mods folder so that you end up with [b]Mods\Tier7Expansion\ModInfo.xml[/b].
[*]Start the game without EasyAntiCheat.
[/list]

Works on an existing save. To uninstall, delete the folder - but a quality 7 item you are holding keeps that quality with no tier 7 defined for it any more, so it loses the bonuses and the slot the tier gave it until the mod is back.

[size=4][b]Multiplayer[/b][/size]

Not client side: the mod changes item definitions, and both ends read those from their own files, so the [b]server and every client need the same version[/b]. Only tested in single player so far - feedback welcome.

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

If another mod has already changed an item's numbers, this mod leaves them as that mod set them rather than overwriting: each of its changes only applies where it finds exactly the value the unmodded game has, and is skipped otherwise.

Overhauls that rebalance weapons and armour wholesale, such as Darkness Falls or Undead Legacy, change those numbers everywhere - expect most of this mod not to apply there.

